{"t":12242,"seq":31,"layer":"acp-in","provider":"goose","conversationId":"sess-d94a0e59-800c-4e8f-9b5e-6b95058c3ebd","runId":"run-66094513-a5ff-45cd-8ea9-c2cc463c1004","data":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_message_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"Here is a haiku about the ocean in traditional 5-7-5 syllable format:\n\n**Waves crash on the shore** (5 syllables)  \n**Endless blue depths hold secrets** (7 syllables)  \n**Salt spray kisses skin** (5 syllables)\n\nThis haiku captures the ocean's power through the imagery of crashing waves, its mystery through the reference to hidden depths and secrets, and its sensory beauty through the gentle touch of salt spray. The traditional syllable pattern creates the rhythmic flow that mirrors the ocean's own eternal rhythm.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:** I wrote a traditional 5-7-5 syllable haiku about the ocean that captures its power (crashing waves), mystery (endless depths with secrets), and sensory beauty (salt spray). The haiku follows proper syllable count and evokes the ocean's essence through vivid imagery."}}}
